A journalist has become the latest victim of an AI-generated deepfake video, a fabricated clip showing him saying or doing things he never did. This is no longer only a social media problem. Deepfakes built with GANs, diffusion models and voice cloning can now fake video and audio almost perfectly, damaging reputations and threatening press freedom. Pakistan's laws have not kept pace. Neither the PPC nor PECA 2016 defines deepfakes, the Qanun-e-Shahadat Order 1984 has no mechanism to detect AI manipulation, and there is no national AI forensic lab to verify such content. Victims are left relying on outdated provisions on defamation, offences against dignity and impersonation. The journalist should not stay silent. He should first preserve the original video, its links and screenshots, then file a complaint with the National Cyber Crime Investigation Agency (formerly the FIA Cybercrime Wing) and request removal through the PTA. He should also file a constitutional petition in the Islamabad High Court under Article 199. That petition can seek directions for urgent takedown, a forensic examination of the video, and a proper investigation, and can invoke his constitutional rights to dignity (Article 14) and free expression (Article 19). Such a case could also push the courts and Parliament toward clear deepfake legislation, so that no journalist or citizen has to face synthetic defamation without protection. 
